# Step 3: a full backup of the whole container database with RMAN.
#
#   rman target / cmdfile=rman/01_backup.rman
#
# RMAN is Oracle's own backup tool and it is the one that matters here, because
# it understands the file formats. A filesystem copy of an open database catches
# datafiles mid-write and produces a backup that cannot be opened; RMAN reads
# through the instance, checks every block as it goes, and records what it did in
# the control file so that a later RESTORE knows what exists.

SHOW ALL;

# Two channels because the container has eight cores and the backup is the
# slowest step in the assignment.
CONFIGURE DEVICE TYPE DISK PARALLELISM 2;

# With autobackup on, RMAN writes a copy of the control file and the spfile at
# the end of every backup. Without it a recovery that has lost the control file
# has to be talked through the backup set by hand.
CONFIGURE CONTROLFILE AUTOBACKUP ON;

CONFIGURE RETENTION POLICY TO RECOVERY WINDOW OF 7 DAYS;

# What is being backed up, before backing it up.
REPORT SCHEMA;

# PLUS ARCHIVELOG archives the current redo log first, includes the archived
# logs in the backup, and archives again afterwards. That is what makes the
# backup usable on its own: the datafile copies are inconsistent, because the
# database was open while they were read, and the redo in the same backup is
# what reconciles them.
BACKUP AS COMPRESSED BACKUPSET DATABASE PLUS ARCHIVELOG;

LIST BACKUP SUMMARY;

# Reads every block of every backup piece and checks it, so that the recovery
# later on is not the first time anyone finds out whether the backup is sound.
VALIDATE DATABASE;

REPORT NEED BACKUP;
